# The comment-drift state machine, locally and without a model.
#
# It is docs/working/comment-drift.tony's first half — head-moved, gather, review — with one
# thing replaced: the review spawns a BROKERED task instead of an llm one, so the drive script
# is the worker. `verse task do complete <id> '<answer>'` supplies the findings, which means the
# answer is a fixture and the TIMING is the script's: a review lands exactly when the test says
# it does.
#
# That is the whole point. The two defects this exists to reproduce are about ORDER, not about
# review quality:
#
#   - a review that finishes AFTER the head moved writes `reviewed: done` over the pending state
#     the new head left, and that commit is never reviewed;
#   - a push of several commits only ever reviews the tip, so a fix in any other commit of it is
#     never seen and its finding stays open forever.
#
# Neither is reachable in under ten minutes against staging, and both are reachable in seconds
# here. The rules below are otherwise VERBATIM from the real charter, so a fix proved here is a
# fix that transfers — when they diverge, this file is the one that is wrong.
at: verse-dev.drift
reads: [ !verse-path [git] ]
---
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: git

- name: drift
  level: 10
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, head]

- name: ledger
  level: 20
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, finding]
---
# 1. Each tip main has had, as a fact this charter owns.
#
# A RECORD PER COMMIT, which is the change under test. `key: $subject.value.sha` makes the
# derive's address a function of (rule, subject, KEY) rather than (rule, subject), so every sha
# gets its own record — and with it its own `reviewed` state, its own subject for rule 3 to
# match, and therefore its own task id.
#
# One slot was the cause of both defects the harness holds still. A late `onDone` wrote
# `reviewed: done` over the state a newer commit had just left, and a push of two overwrote the
# first commit's record before anything could review it: neither has anywhere to land now,
# because neither commit's record is the other's.
#
# The deletes go with it: a fresh record starts empty, so there is no previous review to clear.
- name: head-moved
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject: git:head:verse
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [verse-dev, drift, head]
      key: $subject.value.sha
      value:
        repo: $subject.value.repo
        sha: $subject.value.sha
        reviewed: pending
---
# 2. Before the review, the ledger is read onto the subject.
- name: gather
  stratum: drift
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, head, "*"]
      value:
        reviewed: pending
  action:
    patch:
      reviewed: gathered
      outstanding: !list-paths
        at: [verse-dev, drift, finding, "*"]
        value:
          status: open
---
# 3. …and the tip is reviewed, once, against what is already known.
#
# The spawn is brokered rather than llm: no runner, no environment, no prompt worth reading —
# the drive script claims the task and completes it with the findings the test wants. Everything
# after the spawn (the hooks, the derive, the latch) is the real charter's.
- name: comment-drift
  stratum: drift
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, head, "*"]
      value:
        reviewed: gathered
  action:
    spawn:
      vector: auto
      prompt: |
        Review $sha. The drive script answers this task; nothing here reads the prompt.
    onDone:
      derive:
      - path: [verse-dev, drift, finding]
        value: !each
          over: $data.findings
          key: $item.symbol
          value:
            symbol: $item.symbol
            status: $item.status
            says: $item.says
            at: $item.at
      patch:
        err: !delete null
        result: $data
        reviewed: done
        reviewedSha: $data.at
    onFailed:
      patch:
        err: $err
        result: !delete null
        reviewed: failed
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        err: !delete null
        result: !delete null
        reviewed: cancelled
